# WHY THIS SPEC EXISTS
#
# SyncEngine uses an AIMD (Additive Increase / Multiplicative Decrease)
# controller to adapt how many outbox items are pushed per sync cycle. On
# success it grows by 50%; on failure it halves. This is good for throughput,
# but there is a latent integer-division hazard:
#
#   Math.floor(1 / 2) == 0    →  would freeze sync permanently
#
# The clamp to config.min (default 1) is the only guard. This spec exhaustively
# tests every success/failure interleaving to prove the clamp fires every time
# batch_size would otherwise reach zero.
#
# WHAT WE MODEL
#
# ConcurrencyController:
#   onSuccess  — grow by 50% but by at least 1, clamp to max (20)
#   onFailure  — divide by 2, clamp to min (1)
#
# FizzBee fires both actions in any order, any number of times. We do not
# use `require` guards so the checker freely mixes success/failure sequences.
#
# The "by at least 1" floor is not cosmetic: proportional growth alone stalls at
# MIN_SIZE, because 1 * 3 // 2 == 1. Neither assertion below catches that (the
# batch size stays in bounds and non-zero while sync crawls at one item per
# cycle), and this model mirrored the stall until review caught it in the
# TypeScript. Recovery is a liveness property over a two-element domain, so it is
# asserted exhaustively in tests/unit/services/sync/SyncEngine.concurrency.test.ts
# rather than modelled with history state here.
#
# MODELS (paths and constants checked by tests/guards/spec-drift.test.ts)
#
#   app/services/sync/ConcurrencyController.ts — the class this mirrors; its
#     default config supplies MIN_SIZE, MAX_SIZE and the initial batch_size
#
# WHY A ROLE
#
# The batch size lives in a Controller role rather than as a top-level state
# variable. Both forms verify identically (13 states), but fizzbee-mbt only
# compares system-under-test state through a role adapter — implementing
# getState() on a top-level model adapter crashes the MBT server. Keeping the
# state in a role is what lets formal_verification/mbt/ drive the real
# ConcurrencyController through this state space. See section 9 of
# site/docs/development/walkthroughs/formal-verification.md.
#
# WHAT WE PROVE
#
#   BatchSizeInBounds — batch_size is always in [MIN_SIZE, MAX_SIZE] under all
#                       interleavings
#   NeverZero         — batch_size is never zero (sync would freeze)
#
# NOTE ON INTEGER ARITHMETIC
#
# FizzBee has no floats. `batch_size * 3 // 2` (floor division) is the integer
# equivalent of TypeScript's `Math.floor(batchSize * 1.5)`.
#
# The bounds are top-level constants, not state variables: they are read-only
# config in ConcurrencyController.ts, and keeping them out of the state keeps
# the state space to the reachable batch sizes alone.

MIN_SIZE = 1    # config.min
MAX_SIZE = 20   # config.max

role Controller:
  action Init:
    self.batch_size = 5   # config.initial in ConcurrencyController.ts

  # onSuccess(): grow by 50% but never by less than 1, stay within max.
  atomic action OnSuccess:
    self.batch_size = min(MAX_SIZE, max(self.batch_size + 1, self.batch_size * 3 // 2))

  # onFailure(): halve, stay within min — this is the critical clamp.
  atomic action OnFailure:
    self.batch_size = max(MIN_SIZE, self.batch_size // 2)

action Init:
  controller = Controller()


always assertion BatchSizeInBounds:
  return controller.batch_size >= MIN_SIZE and controller.batch_size <= MAX_SIZE

always assertion NeverZero:
  return controller.batch_size >= 1
